Sunlight-driven splitting of water into ${\sf H}_{2}$ has attracted intensive attentions because its potential application to solve energy and environmental problems. $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ is the well-known semiconductor-type photocatalyst most studied in this area. However, due to the large bandgap it just absorbs ultraviolet light with energy accounting for only $3-5\%$ of solar spectrum.1b Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of nanoporous materials in which inorganic building units are connected by organic linker via coordination bonding.2 Recently, by encoding Ti-oxo clusters within a MOF is demonstrated as a promising approach to achieve a photocatalyst with more efficient solar energy conversion, wherein the MOF exhibits semiconductor behaviors with electrons transferring from the photo excited organic linkers to the metal-oxo clusters.3-4 The large surface areas and downsized catalytic centers (e.g. small metal-oxo cluster) of a MOF that are beneficial for an efficient photocatalyst. More importantly, the bandgap and photoactivity of a MOF can been rationally tuned by simply varying the organic linkers.5-9 For example, the bandgap of MIL-125-NH2 significantly decreased as a 2-aminoterephthalate linker was used to replace the terephthalate linker in MIL-125 (a MOF assembled with Ti-oxo cluster and terephthalate linker).9 Thus, the amino-functionalized $M L-125-N H_{2}$ was successfully used as visible-light-induced photocatalysts for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction10 and ${\sf H}_{2}$ production11 that can’t be achieved by MIL-125. García and co-workers also demonstrated the amino-functionalized ${\mathsf{U i O}}{\mathsf{-}}66{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ (a MOF derived from Zr-oxo cluster and 2-aminoterephthalate linker) produced a much bathochromic shift in the optical spectrum as compared to its analogous UiO-66 (containing terephthalate linker).12 Inspired by this fact, people also chosen other substituted groups such as –Br, -OH, -SH and $-N O_{2}$ to tune the organic linker with the aim to achieve the visible-light-driven photocatalysts based on MOF.13 However, the $-N H_{2}$ is evidenced as the most efficient functional group by use such method till now.9-13
With the aim to explore new visible-light-promoted photocatalyst with higher catalytic ${\sf H}_{2}$ production, herein we report the investigation of the photocatalytic property of hybrid materials derived from MIL-125 by use methylthio group as a new type of functional group on the terephthalic acid linker (abbreviated as $H_{2}B D C-(S C H_{3})_{2})$ (Scheme 1). The methylthio group $(-S C H_{3})$ is expected to donate the S 3p electrons to the aromatic linking unit, acting as auxochromic and bathochromic substitute on aromatic ring to extend the absorption band to visible-light region. Direct synthesis of MOF MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ with $H_{2}B D C-(S C H_{3})_{2}$ was failed. While, hybrid $\times\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ complexes were obtained through a solvent-assisted ligand exchange (SALE) process14 with BDC- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ replacing BDC linker (terephthalate) of the parent MIL-125 (Scheme 1 and ESI, $x\%$ is the ratio of BDC- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ exchanged into MOF’s framework). Two hybrid materials containing $20\%$ and $50\%$ BDC- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ exchanged linkers were obtained, which are denoted as $20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ and $50\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ respectively. As expected, the $\times\%-M I L-125-(S C H_{3})_{2}$ showed visible region absorb up to $525\mathsf{n m}$ and bandgap lowered to ${\sim}2.6$ eV, being in sharp contrast to that main absorption in UV region (345nm) and that high gap value (ca. 3.6 eV) of its parent MIL125.9a To our pleasant surprise, by use Pt as co-catalyst the highest ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution rate of $P t/\times\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ under visible-light irradiation $(\lambda>400\ \mathsf{n m}$ , sacrificial triethanolamine) is up to $3814.0~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}{\cdot\mathsf{g}^{-1}}{\cdot\mathsf{h}^{-1}}$ , and the high quantum yield (QE) at $420\ \mathsf{n m}$ is up to $8.90\%$ . The photocatalytic performance of which even is one order of magnitude higher than that of the state-ofthe-art $\mathsf{P t/M I L-}125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ photocatalyst derived from 2- aminoterephthalate,11 being the most visible-light photoactive MOF’s material for ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution from water to our best knowledge.
$20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ and $50\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ were obtained by mixing $H_{2}B D C-(S C H_{3})_{2}$ and MIL-125 in DMF and varying the $H_{2}B D C-(S C H_{3})_{2}$ : BDC (based on $\mathsf{T i}_{8}\mathsf{O}_{8}({\mathsf{O H}})_{4}({\mathsf{B D C}}_{2})_{6}$ formula of MIL-125) molar ratios (i. e., 2 :1 and $4:1$ , see ESI). The resulted solids were soaked in DMF for 3d, with the solution replaced by fresh DMF $10m L)$ every $24\ h$ to remove the $H_{2}B D C-(S C H_{3})_{2}$ adsorbed on the solid surface, and further washed by methanol to remove the residual DMF. The treated solids were desolvated and subjected to $\mathsf{\Omega}^{1}\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectroscopy analyses (600MHz, $D C//C$ -DMSO), which showed a total of $20\%$ and $50\%$ of BDC linkers in MIL-125 were replaced by BDC$(S C H_{3})_{2}$ , respectively (Fig. 1a). Powder X-ray diffraction PXRD patterns of $20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ and $50\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ match well with those of the parent MIL-125 (Fig. 1b), inferring the entitle structure remained intact after the BDC linker partically exchanged. The evaluated Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (SBET) from ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms show gas adsorption of $1332.38~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ , $1101.57\mathrm{m}^{2}/9$ and $737.09\mathrm{m}^{2}/9\$ for MIL-125, $20\%$ - MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ and $50\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ , respectively (Fig. 1c). The 1 nm pore-size distribution decreased with increasing amount $x\%$ of BDC- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ (Fig. 1d), while that of $0.8\ \mathsf{n m}$ increased, indicating the $-S C H_{3}$ group of BDC- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ protruding to the empty space and thus decreased the pore sizes and BET of $\times\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}a S$ compared to their parent MIL-125. These fact confirm that BDC- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ ligands replaced the BDC ligands within the MIL-125, rather than aggregated on the outer surface. Two characteristic peaks of $\subset-$ H vibration band of $-C H_{3}$ around $2934~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ and S-C at 1254 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ of BDC- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ ligands (Fig. S1 in ESI) of solid FT-IR spectrometry for $x\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ also suggest the successful ligand exchange.
$(S C H_{3})_{2}$ before and after H2 production (b), ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms (c) and pore-size distributions (d).
Surface morphology of $\times\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ is a pill-like form similar to that of the parent $M1L-125^{15\mathrm{a}}$ (Fig. S2). EDSmapping images of the $20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ nanoparticles also show a uniform distribution of C, O, S and Ti elements all over the nanoparticle (Fig. S3), indicating the elements homogeneously distributed. Solid UV−vis spectra were measured and compared to that of MIL-125 and free $H_{2}B D C-$ $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ ligand, as that shown in Fig. 2a. In sharp contrast to that absorption band below 350 nm of MIL-125, intense absorption bands from $350~\mathsf{n m}$ to 550 nm peaking at $420~\mathsf{n m}$ were observed for $\times\%-M H L-125-(S C H_{3})_{2}$ samples. Such broadened absorptions in visible-light region are also reflected in their physical crystallization appearance of yellow color. The color variations infer the electronic band structure around the band gap is significantly modified. Based on the relation $(\mathsf{A h v})^{2}$ $\scriptstyle=\mathtt{q}(\mathsf{h}\lor-\mathsf{E}_{\mathfrak{g}})$ , optical bandgap for $20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ and $50\%$ - MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ are less different, being $2.69~\mathsf{e V}$ and 2.61 eV (Table S2 and Fig. S7), respectively. These values are much lower than $3.8~\mathsf{e V}$ of the parent MIL-125, being slight smaller than that $2.72~\mathsf{e V}^{9\mathsf{a}}$ for the amino-functionalized MIL- $125\cdot N H_{2}$ (Table S2). Theoretical investigations also were performed in order to elucidate the electronic properties of the $\times\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ solids, using HSE06 density functional (see ESI). Figure S6 depicts the valence band (VB) and conduction bond (CB) orbitals for $\times\%-M H L-125-(S C H_{3})_{2}$ and MIL-125. The CB orbitals spread mainly on the Ti atoms and –COO- groups, while VB orbitals are mainly on the benzene rings. Introducing two and six BDC- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ linkers per unit cell (i.e., to give $20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ and $50\%-111L-125-(5C H_{3})_{2})$ break the orginal I4/mmm symmetry and mainly affects the VB, while the CB orbital energy changed little. Delocalized orbitals are formed between S 3p orbitals and the π orbitals of benzene rings. Due to the electrondonating characteristics of the - ${\mathrm{\cdot}}{\mathsf{S C H}}_{3}$ groups, the VB orbital energy of $\times\%-M I L-125-(S C H_{3})_{2}$ thus increase $\sim1.52$ eV as compared to that of MIL-125, leading to the reduced bandgaps $(\sim2.4~\mathsf{e V})$ in $x\%-M\vert(L-125-(S C H_{3})_{2}$ . The observed localized CB and VB frontier orbitals imply there is no long range chemical interaction between BDC- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ , thus increased BDC- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ content would only improve the number of bands near the VB but have little impact on the VB orbital energy, leading to the less different bandgaps between $\times\%-M\vert\vert L-125-(S C H_{3})_{2}$ solids $(\sim2.4~\mathsf{e V})$ .This result is consistent with that similar band gap ${\sim}2.6\ \mathsf{e V}$ observed for $20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ and $50\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ in the experimental study (Table S2, Fig.S7). Therefore, the band gap is independent of the proportion of BDC- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ content in different $\times\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ as that observed for a series of amine functional MIL- $125\cdot N H_{2}$ analogues reported.9a
These above facts indicate that the decoration of BDC$(S C H_{3})_{2}$ linker on $\times\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ could greatly enhance its photo-response capability and photo-generated charge separation that to be used for the photocurrent generation. To provide an additional evidence for this point of view, interfacial properties between the electrode and electrolyte were characterized by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) (see ESI). Fig. 2c shows that the capacitance arc for $20\%$ -MIL$125-(5C H_{3})_{2}$ is much smaller than that of MIL-125, suggesting the electron mobility was improved after the ligand exchanged by BDC- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ , thus the electron-hole recombination rate was effectively prohibited.15b Moreover, impedance spectroscope was performed at an AC frequency of $1\mathsf{K H z}$ to acquire MottSchottky (M-S) plots15c (can see ESI). As shown in Fig. 2d, the positive slopes of M−S plots suggest a n-type semiconductor behavior for both MIL-125 and $20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ . The flat band position $(E_{F B})$ values of MIL-125 and $20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ estimated via Mott-Schottky equation15c,d at $\mathsf{1/C}^{2}=0$ (Fig. 2d and see ESI) are $-0.78{\mathrm{~V~}}$ and $-0.85\vee$ vs. $\mathsf{A g}/\mathsf{A g C l}$ (i.e. - 0.58 V, -0.65 V vs NHE), respectively, thus corresponding conductor band (CB) of which are estimated15e to be $-0.68{\sf V}$ and -0.75 $N$ vs. NHE. CB value of $20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ is more negative than the redox potential of $\mathsf{H}^{+}/\mathsf{H}_{2}$ (-0.41 V vs. NHE, pH ${\bf\omega}=7\mathbf{\gamma}$ ).15f The more negative CB value is theoretically feasible for the photocatalytic hydrogen evolution over $20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ . With the bandgap energy $(\mathsf{E}_{9})$ of MIL-125 and $20\%$ -
MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ estimated to be $3.80~\mathsf{e V}$ and 2.69 eV (Fig. S7), respectively, their valence band (VB) calculated correspondingly thus are $3.12~\mathsf{V}$ and $1.94~\mathsf{V}$ vs. NHE (Fig. 2d). In addition, the carrier density $N_{\mathsf{D}}$ calculated15d for MIL-125 and $20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ are $8.94\times10^{18}$ and 1.14 $\times10^{19}\ c m^{-3}$ , respectively. The higher $N_{\mathsf{D}}$ of the latter indicates that a faster carrier transfer than that in the former.
As known, higher photocurrent intensities are always favorable for driving the photocatalytic process owing to more photogenerated electrons to be utilized. Therefore, the $x\%$ -MIL125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ samples were chosen as a visible-light photocatalyst for driving the ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution from aqueous solution under visible-light irradiation $(\lambda>400\ \mathsf{n m})$ . Parallel experiments via choosing $20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ , Pt/BDC- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ and Ptphotodeposited16c $P t/\times\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ as photocatalysts in comparison were carried out by respectively dispersing their solids $(50~\mathsf{m g})$ in $80~\mathrm{{\mL}}$ aqueous solution with 0.01 M triethanolamine (TEOA) as a sacrificial electron donor (see ESI). As shown in Fig. 3a, the total evolution amount of ${\sf H}_{2}$ after 2 h of reactions are 0, 1.23, $140.50$ , and $116.77~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}$ for $P t/H_{2}B D C-$ $(S C H_{3})_{2},$ $20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ , $P t/20\%-M I L-125-(S C H_{3})_{2}$ and $P t/50\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ , respectively. In comparison to the Pt/MIL-125 reported,11 $P t/\times\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ showed excellent photocatalytic performance, while $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}\cdot(\mathsf{S C H}_{3})_{2}$ exhibited negligible active performance. Obviously, methylthiofunctionalized terephthalate $(B D C-(S C H_{3})_{2})$ linker fabricated within MIL-125 framework is responsible for the markedly improved photocatalytic performance of $\times\%-M I L-125-(S C H_{3})_{2}$ composites for the ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution. In comparison, $50\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ is less stable which was decomposed/degradated under the photocatalytic reaction, while $20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ remained although the PXRD peaks were markedly weaken after 2 hours (Fig. 1b). The relative stability of $20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ is favorable for its photocatalytic performance and thus exhibits the higher activity. Here higher photocatalytic activity of $P t/20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ over that of $P t/50\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ also is reflected in the higher photocurrent of the former (Fig. 2b).
Nevertheless, the sample of $20\%-M1L-125-(S C H_{3})_{2}$ still exhibited a negligible activity for ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution. In the presence of Pt co-catalyst, $P t/20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ exhibited a marked increase of ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution activity, being about 100 times of that of $20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ .These facts suggest the $\mathsf{P t}$ nanoparticles played important role in efficient charge separation, and then enhanced the photocatalytic efficiency of the ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution reaction. High-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) was used to characterize the as-made $P t/20\%$ -MIL
$125-(5C H_{3})_{2}$ obtained via photodeposition process. The Pt nanoparticles with the average size around 1 nm were observed incorporated on the catalyst (Fig.S2c). For excluding the possible effect of the $\Gamma_{1}0_{2}$ resulted from the $\times\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ during photodeposition process, PXRD patterns of $\times\%$ - MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ before and after the $\mathsf{P t}$ photodeposition  show that no characteristic peak of bulk $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ phase was observed (Fig. 1b), suggesting the photodeposition of Pt process did not affect the whole structure of the MOF materials.
Even in comparison to $M L-125-N H_{2}$ ， a state-of-the-art photocatalytic MOF that produced moderate amount of ${\sf H}_{2}$ ,11 the performance of $\times\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ were markedly improved in the presence of $-S C H_{3}$ group. The highest ${\sf H}_{2}$ -evolution rate $(3814.0~\upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\up g^{-1}\cdot\mathsf{h}^{-1})$ calculated from 20 to 40 min for $\mathsf{P t}/20\%$ - MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ is almost one order of magnitude higher than that 366.7 μmol· $\mathfrak{g}^{-1}\cdot\mathfrak{h}^{-1}$ of $P t/M I L-125\ –N H_{2}$ under the same conditions.11 This value also is much higher than these representative photocatalytic MOFs reported. It is over $200\%$ of that of the sensitized $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{g}{\cdot}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}{\cdot}66$ (1411 $\mathsf{\upmu m o l\cdot g}^{-1}\cdot\mathsf{h}^{-1})^{195}$ and Calix-3/Pt@UiO-66-NH2-1-200 $(1528\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\up g^{-1}\cdot\mathsf{h}^{-1})$ ,19f being the most photoactive MOF-based materials to the best of our knowledge (Fig. 3b and Table S3). For better clarifying the photocatalytic performance, the quantum efficiencies $(\mathsf{Q}\mathsf{E})^{17}$ at 420nm of $P t/20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ and $P t/50\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ were calculated to be about $5.99\%$ and $8.90\%$ , respectively (Fig. S5). Such QEs even are much higher than that $(4.3\%)$ of the $Z n\vert n_{2}S_{4}@\mathsf{M}\mathsf{L}.125.\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ (Table S1).18c
A possible mechanism for the photocatalytic process of $p t/x\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ can be explained by the linker BDC$(S C H_{3})_{2}$ acting as functional antennas. As shown in Fig. 4, under light irradiation the organic (BDC- $(S C H_{3})_{2})$ within the lattice of $\times\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ may absorb the visible-light, and the photogenerated electrons will transfer from its excited state to the conduction band (CB) of the Ti-oxo clusters through a linkerto-cluster-charge transfer (LCCT) mechanism,3e generating $T_{1}^{3+}$ and a hole at the organic linker. Then, the Pt cocatalyst spatially separated electrons from the excited BDC- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ , meanwhile the hole reacted with the electron donor TEOA, leading to the restraining of the recombination and the promoting hydrogen evolution on Pt. As that elucidated via theoretical calculation, it is the S 3p donating electron density to the $\pi$ -orbitals of the benzene ring of terephthalate linker, which results in a much higher HOMO level ( $1.94\mathrm{~V~}$ of $20\%-M1L-125(S C H_{3})_{2}$ vs. 3.12 V of MIL-125, Fig. 2d) and thus narrows the band gaps to $\sim2.60$ eV. Herein, the similar band gaps $(\sim2.60~\mathsf{e V})$ of $20\%$ -MIL$125(\mathsf{S C H}_{3})_{2}$ and $50\%-M1L-125(S C H_{3})_{2}$ are lower as compared to that $2.72\ \mathsf{e V}$ of MIL- $125-N H_{2}$ (Table S2). The Hammett constant represents the effect of different substituents on the electronic character of a given aromatic system. A negative Hammet $\upsigma_{\mathfrak{m}}$ indicates an electron-donating. The lower band gap of $\times\%$ -MIL$125(\mathsf{S C H}_{3})_{2}$ compared to that of MIL-125-NH2 may be abscribed to the disubstituted variant of BDC- $(\mathsf{S C H}_{3})_{2}$ , although the Hammet $\upsigma_{\mathfrak{m}}$ order of subtituents is $\begin{array}{r}{\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\left.-N\mathsf{H}_{2}<-\mathsf{S C H}_{3}.\right.}\end{array}$ 13 The lowered band gap bring absorption to the visible region,9,12 and lead to the enhanced photocatalytic performance of $x\%$ -MIL$125(\mathsf{S C H}_{3})_{2}$ . However, when methanol $(20\%,\upnu/\upnu)$ , EDTA (0.01 M) and aniline (0.01 M) were used as sacrificial electron donors, $p t/20\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ exhibited no promotion for ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution, while as Vitamin C $(0.3~\mathsf{m M})$ ) or TEA (0.01 M) sacrificial reagents were used under the same condition, a much low photacatalytic performance $(66.7\upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\up g^{-1}\cdot\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ , $124.3\ \mu\ m o l\cdot\ g^{-1}\cdot\mathsf h^{-1}$ , Fig. S8) were observed. $p t/20\%-M I L-125-(S C H_{3})_{2}$ was also decomposed/degradated for TEA sacrificial case as that observed for the TEOA sacrificial reagent after two hours of photocatalystic reaction, which suggests the weak stability of $\times\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ under basic condition. No photoactivity observed for the methanol etc. sacrificial reagents indicates the relative weak oxidation ability of the excited organic linker BDC$(S C H_{3})_{2}$ during the photocatalytic reaction.
We conclude this article by highlighting the advantage of use methylthio-functionalized MOF to achieve an efficient visible-light-driven photocatalyst. Herein, the photocatalytic activity of hybrid $\times\%$ -MIL-125- $(S C H_{3})_{2}$ containing the 2,5- bis(methylthio)terephthalate (BDC- $(S C H_{3})_{2})$ was markedly improved as even compared to that well-known aminofunctionalized MIL- $125\cdot N H_{2}$ under the same condition, being the most photocatalytic MOF-base materials driven by visible light. And the QE value is up to $8.90\%$ at $420\ \mathsf{n m}$ as splitting water into $H_{2}$ . This work open a new way up to obtain more efficient MOF’ photocatalyst with visible-light response, and such type of thioether-functionalized MOF is worthy of further exploring.
